1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
Listen to a talk in an economics class.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:06,800
Imagine that you have a full time job,

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,170
but you want to take a year off to go back to school.

4
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:11,002
What is it going to cost you?

5
00:00:11,002 --> 00:00:15,210
Of course there's the cost of tuition for your classes, plus living expenses.

6
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:18,753
But what about the money you're no longer earning at your job

7
00:00:18,753 --> 00:00:20,229
because you're now a student?

8
00:00:20,229 --> 00:00:22,000
We call that the opportunity cost.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,696
Opportunity cost refers to the value of the next best alternative that must be

10
00:00:25,696 --> 00:00:27,280
given up when making a choice.

11
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:33,284
It's a fundamental principle in economics that helps individuals and businesses understand the true

12
00:00:33,284 --> 00:00:35,000
cost of their decisions.

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,308
My exercise in imagination illustrates a personal decision,

14
00:00:38,308 --> 00:00:42,030
but businesses also face opportunity costs in their operations.

15
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:46,307
For instance, a company may choose to invest in new equipment rather than spend

16
00:00:46,307 --> 00:00:48,140
money to expand its product line.

17
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:52,487
The opportunity cost of this decision is the potential revenue that could have been

18
00:00:52,487 --> 00:00:54,040
generated from the new products.

19
00:00:54,040 --> 00:01:00,600
Understanding opportunity cost helps individuals as well as businesses make informed decisions that maximize their benefits

20
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,240
and minimize their losses.

21
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:07,020
It's important to note that opportunity cost is not always measured in financial terms.

22
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:12,050
It can also be related to factors like time, convenience or personal satisfaction.

23
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:18,200
For that reason, opportunity cost can be difficult to quantify, especially when dealing with non monetary factors.

24
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,100
Have you ever had to make a decision that included an opportunity cost?

25
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:26,080
Take a few minutes now to think and then I'll ask for volunteers to share.
